  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.dispersion - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.dispersion.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.dispersion()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate dispersion between two nodes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.dispersion()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate dispersion between two nodes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.dispersion()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Calculate dispersion between two nodes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCalculate dispersion between two nodes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L35790-L35944){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.dispersion(\n    node_1,\n    node_2,\n    normalized=True,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Dispersion](../assets/graphs/dispersion.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Nodes A and B are outlined in the accent color and share three neighbors C, D, and E, filled in accent. C and D are linked, but E touches neither, so two of the three shared-neighbor pairs are unlinked: dispersion 2 / 3 = 0.667.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nDispersion measures the embeddedness of a link in the network. A link between two nodes\n(`node_1` and `node_2`) has high dispersion when their mutual neighbors (friends in\ncommon) are not well connected to each other. This can indicate structural diversity in\nthe network.\n\nThe computation first collects the common neighbors of `node_1` and `node_2`. It then\nlooks at every pair `(s, t)` of those common neighbors and counts the pair as\n*dispersed* when `s` and `t` are not adjacent and also have no common neighbor among the\nother neighbors of `node_1` (i.e., they are more than two hops apart in `node_1`'s ego\nnetwork once `node_1` and `node_2` are set aside). The raw dispersion is the number of\ndispersed pairs; the normalized version divides it by the number of common neighbors.\n\nDispersion is useful for:\n\n- identifying especially strong or intimate ties, such as partners or close family,\n  which tend to connect otherwise separate social circles\n- distinguishing ties that sit inside one tight cluster from ties that span several\n- complementing plain overlap measures like `common_neighbors()`, which can't tell these\n  two situations apart\n\nThings to keep in mind: `node_1` and `node_2` do not need to be adjacent; if they have\nfewer than two common neighbors the result is `0.0`. The measure is designed for\nundirected graphs. For directed graphs, neighbors are the outgoing neighbors of each\nnode. The cost grows with the square of the number of common neighbors, which is\nnegligible for typical pairs.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[node_1]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The first node of the pair, given as a node ID (`int`) or label (`str`).\n\n<code>[node_2]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The second node of the pair, given as a node ID (`int`) or label (`str`). The result\n    is not symmetric in `node_1` and `node_2`: distances are measured among `node_1`'s\n    neighbors, so swapping the two can change it.\n\n<code>[normalized]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (the default), divide the count of dispersed pairs by the number of common\n    neighbors of `node_1` and `node_2` (their embeddedness), which makes scores easier to\n    compare across ties with different numbers of mutual friends. If `False`, return the\n    raw count of dispersed pairs (as a float).\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The dispersion score between `node_1` and `node_2`. `0.0` when they share fewer than\n    two neighbors or when all their shared neighbors are closely connected.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `node_1` or `node_2` does not exist in the graph.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nDispersion, introduced by Backstrom and Kleinberg (2014), asks a subtler question\nthan raw neighbor overlap: given the common neighbors of two nodes, how *dispersed*\nare they across the rest of the network? If the shared neighbors are themselves\ntightly interlinked, the tie between $u$ and $v$ sits in a dense cluster and\ndispersion is low; if those neighbors are far from one another in the residual\ngraph, the tie spans different regions and dispersion is high. That property made it\nstrikingly effective in its original application, spotting romantic partners in\nsocial-network data, where such ties bridge otherwise distinct friendship circles\neven when embeddedness is high.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~common_neighbors`) \n\n:   The number of shared neighbors of two nodes.\n\n[](`~local_bridges`) \n\n:   Edges whose ends share no neighbor at all.\n\n[](`~clustering_coefficient`) \n\n:   How well a node's neighbors are linked to each other.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\n`U` and `V` share three neighbors: `A`, `B` and `C`. `A` and `B` know each other, but `C`\nis linked to neither, so two of the three pairs of shared neighbors (`A`–`C` and `B`–`C`)\nare dispersed, giving `2 / 3`. With `normalized=False`, the count of dispersed pairs:\n\n::: {#33e379ee .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"U\", \"V\"), (\"U\", \"A\"), (\"U\", \"B\"), (\"U\", \"C\"),\n     (\"V\", \"A\"), (\"V\", \"B\"), (\"V\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"B\")]\n)\n\n(\n    round(graph.dispersion(\"U\", \"V\"), 3),\n    graph.dispersion(\"U\", \"V\", normalized=False),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(0.667, 2.0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nPairs are judged among the neighbors of the first node. A friend `X` of `U` who knows\nboth `B` and `C` links that pair outside the `U`–`V` tie, so it no longer counts and the\ndispersion of `U` with `V` drops to `1 / 3`. Seen from `V`, who doesn't know `X`, it stays\nat `2 / 3`:\n\n::: {#0146ebc3 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.add_node(label=\"X\")\ngraph.add_edge(\"U\", \"X\").add_edge(\"B\", \"X\").add_edge(\"C\", \"X\")\n\nround(graph.dispersion(\"U\", \"V\"), 3), round(graph.dispersion(\"V\", \"U\"), 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(0.333, 0.667)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Spotting a couple\n\n`friends()` holds the friendships among 24 members of two clubs. Ranking every friendship\nby dispersion, one stands far above the rest:\n\n::: {#31b41858 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nfriends = ec.friends()\nlabel = friends.get_label_from_id\n\npl.DataFrame(\n    [\n        {\n            \"friend_1\": label(u), \"friend_2\": label(v),\n            \"dispersion\": friends.dispersion(u, v),\n        }\n        for _, u, v in friends.get_edges_as_tuples()\n    ]\n).sort(\"dispersion\", descending=True).head(4)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (4, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>friend_1</th><th>friend_2</th><th>dispersion</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>str</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Hugo&quot;</td><td>&quot;Tara&quot;</td><td>1.5</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Ben&quot;</td><td>&quot;Cleo&quot;</td><td>0.5</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Gwen&quot;</td><td>&quot;Kemal&quot;</td><td>0.5</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Iris&quot;</td><td>&quot;Kemal&quot;</td><td>0.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nHugo, from the chess club, and Tara, from the climbing club, are a couple. They share\nfour friends, two from each club, and no two of the four know each other, so all six pairs\nare dispersed:\n\n::: {#d0f806c6 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nhugo, tara = friends.get_neighbors(\"Hugo\"), friends.get_neighbors(\"Tara\")\nshared = set(hugo) & set(tara)\n\nfriends.plot(\n    color_by=\"club\",\n    highlight={\"accent\": [\"Hugo\", \"Tara\"], \"soft\": list(shared)},\n    label_by=\"label\",\n    seed=1,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-4df429d6:#fbdbc3;--ect-7fed765d:#af9988;--ect-04f10aa5:#c5d2e2;--ect-bf2d28a2:#89939e;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-4df429d6:#493426;--ect-7fed765d:#33241a;--ect-04f10aa5:#1f2d3e;--ect-bf2d28a2:#151f2b;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-4df429d6:#fbdbc3;--ect-7fed765d:#af9988;--ect-04f10aa5:#c5d2e2;--ect-bf2d28a2:#89939e;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-4df429d6:#493426;--ect-7fed765d:#33241a;--ect-04f10aa5:#1f2d3e;--ect-bf2d28a2:#151f2b;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"14.0 -18.6 780.2 557.3\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'S